{%- comment -%}
  THE shared product card. Used by the homepage bestsellers row, the PLP +
  related rows — so every change here is catalogue-wide.

  Params:
    product     required
    card_index  optional, 1-based position in the calling grid. Card 1 gets
                fetchpriority=high (it is the mobile LCP element on /collections/*),
                cards 2–4 stay eager (browser default, first two mobile rows),
                5+ lazy. No index passed = lazy, the safe default.

  Anatomy:
    .card__media  the photograph, the hover second shot, and the two text tags
                  (sale / sold out). NOTHING ELSE SITS ON THE PHOTOGRAPH.
    .card__body   vendor (optional) → NAME → price → quick-add → swatches.

  QUICK-ADD IS OFF THE PHOTOGRAPH. It used to be a bare 19px bag glyph pinned
  bottom-right INSIDE the image, drawn in ink with no plate behind it. On this
  catalogue — ivory garments shot on limestone — the glyph landed on near-white
  for most of the grid and simply was not there. A control a shopper cannot see
  is not a control. It is now `.card__add` in `.card__body`, a real labelled
  target on paper, which the stylesheet can pin, reveal on hover or show
  permanently without ever having to guess the brightness of a photograph.

  THE TAGS ARE TEXT, NOT BOXES. A solid red chip is the visual language of a
  discount marketplace; it is the single fastest way to make a considered label
  look like a clearance rail. Same information, set as small tracked type:
  `.card__tag--sale` top-left when the product has a REAL discount and is
  buyable, `.card__tag--sold` in the same corner when it is not (they can never
  collide — a "Sale" tag on a piece you cannot buy is noise). The percentage
  is NOT repeated up there; it prints once, in the price line.

  NAME = the full product.title, pipe included, shown as-is; uppercase comes
  from the stylesheet, never from the data.

  PRICE: one formatting rule for the whole theme, `snippets/price.liquid`,
  driven by settings.price_show_cents (price-badge and the PDP go through it
  too now, and money() in theme.js/cart-extras.js reads the same setting via
  Theme.showCents — so the server-rendered and JS-repainted halves of the store
  agree). The card still does not render the shared `price-badge` component:
  its markup is shared with the PDP, which is why the card could never give the
  name and the price separate weights. The three parts are now named elements
  (.card__price-was / -now / -off) so hierarchy is a stylesheet decision.

  GLOBAL SETTINGS this card reads (all via `| default:`, so the card renders
  correctly on a store whose settings_data.json predates them):
    card_image_ratio  portrait/2:3 (default) · tall/3:4 · square/1:1 ·
                      4:5 · natural — the schema ships the word forms
    card_hover        'second' (default) shows product.images[1] on hover
    card_show_vendor  off by default — vendor is inconsistently filled on most
                      catalogues, and a wrong-looking line under every name is
                      worse than no line
    card_quick_add    off hides .card__add entirely
    price_show_cents  see snippets/price.liquid

  theme.js contract (do not rename): [data-quick-add-root] wraps the card so the
  pointerover prefetch finds [data-quick-view]; [data-quick-add] carries either
  data-single-variant (adds straight to the bag) or data-quick-view=<handle>
  (opens the quick-view dialog); [data-swatch] dots are painted from
  qvSwatchMap by paintSwatches(), which runs once against the whole document
  on load and again against just the injected grid on the related row — it
  finds every dot here regardless of where in the card markup it lives.

  card-extras.js contract (do not rename): `.card__media` must contain
  `.card__art img` (the lead photograph it swaps on swatch preview), and
  `.card__art` and `.card__info` must both stay <a> elements — it repoints both
  hrefs at the selected variant.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  assign card_name = product.title

  assign has_discount = false
  if product.compare_at_price > product.price
    assign has_discount = true
    assign card_pct = product.compare_at_price | minus: product.price | times: 100.0 | divided_by: product.compare_at_price | round
  endif

  assign card_cat = product.type
  if card_cat == blank and product.collections.size > 0
    assign card_cat = product.collections.first.title
  endif

  comment
    ---- global card settings, each with a fallback ----
    The ratio is handed to the stylesheet BOTH ways: `data-ratio` for the
    per-value rules, and `--card-ratio` as the literal aspect-ratio value, so a
    ratio the stylesheet has no rule for still crops correctly instead of
    falling back to a square. The same numbers go on the <img> width/height
    attributes: those reserve the box before the image lands, and a card grid
    with the wrong intrinsic ratio is one of the few places a theme can still
    ship layout shift on a fast connection.
  endcomment
  comment
    The `when` arms carry BOTH the ratio-shaped spellings and the schema's own
    option values ('portrait' / 'tall' / 'square' in settings_schema.json).
    Keep them together: an option value with no arm here falls through to the
    `else` and silently renders 2:3, and because the inline --card-ratio on
    .card overrides the :root token, the wrong crop wins over the correct one
    the layout injected. That is exactly how 'tall' shipped broken once.
  endcomment
  assign card_ratio = settings.card_image_ratio | default: '2:3'
  assign card_w = 600
  case card_ratio
    when '1:1', '1_1', 'square'
      assign card_h = 600
    when '4:5', '4_5'
      assign card_h = 750
    when '3:4', '3_4', 'tall'
      assign card_h = 800
    when '2:3', '2_3', 'portrait'
      assign card_h = 900
    when 'natural', 'adapt', 'image'
      comment
        The image's own ratio — no --card-ratio is emitted, the intrinsic
        attributes are the image's real pixels, and the stylesheet must not
        force an aspect-ratio for this value.
      endcomment
      assign card_w = product.featured_image.width | default: 600
      assign card_h = product.featured_image.height | default: 900
    else
      assign card_h = 900
  endcase

  comment
    'second' is the default because the hover cross-fade is what the theme
    already shipped; 'none' (or any other value) renders no second image at
    all, which also removes the byte cost of a second photograph per card.
  endcomment
  assign card_hover = settings.card_hover | default: 'second'

  assign card_show_vendor = settings.card_show_vendor | default: false

  comment
    card_quick_add may reach us as a checkbox (true/false) or as a select
    ('button'/'none'), depending on how it is declared in settings_schema.json
    — this card is not the owner of that file. Both shapes are handled: a
    `| default:` alone would turn an unchecked checkbox (false) back ON, which
    is the one outcome a merchant would notice as a bug.
  endcomment
  assign card_quick_add = settings.card_quick_add | default: 'button'
  assign show_quick_add = true
  if card_quick_add == 'none' or card_quick_add == 'off' or settings.card_quick_add == false
    assign show_quick_add = false
  endif

  assign card_swatch_option = null
  assign card_swatch_index = -1
  for option in product.options_with_values
    assign opt_name = option.name | downcase
    if opt_name == 'color' or opt_name == 'colour'
      assign card_swatch_option = option
      comment
        index is needed by the swatch photo fallback below (variant.options[i]) —
        same lookup main-product.liquid does for the PDP colour picker.
      endcomment
      assign card_swatch_index = forloop.index0
    endif
  endfor
-%}
<article
  class="card"
  data-quick-add-root
  data-ratio="{{ card_ratio | escape }}"
  data-hover="{{ card_hover | escape }}"
  {%- unless card_ratio == 'natural' or card_ratio == 'adapt' or card_ratio == 'image' %} style="--card-ratio: {{ card_w }} / {{ card_h }};"{% endunless -%}
>
  <div class="card__media">
    <a class="card__art" href="{{ product.url }}" aria-label="{{ product.title | escape }}" tabindex="-1">
      {%- if product.featured_image -%}
        <img
          src="{{ product.featured_image | image_url: width: 640 }}"
          srcset="{{ product.featured_image | image_url: width: 420 }} 420w, {{ product.featured_image | image_url: width: 640 }} 640w, {{ product.featured_image | image_url: width: 900 }} 900w"
          sizes="(max-width: 720px) 50vw, 25vw"
          width="{{ card_w }}" height="{{ card_h }}"
          alt="{{ product.featured_image.alt | default: product.title | escape }}"
          {%- if card_index == 1 %}
            fetchpriority="high"
          {%- elsif card_index and card_index <= 4 %}
          {%- else %}
            loading="lazy"
          {%- endif %}
        >
        {%- comment -%} The hover image is decoration for pointer devices only, so
        the card stylesheet sets `display:none` under (hover: none) — a lazy image
        with no box never intersects and is therefore never fetched on a phone at
        all. It also carries the SAME srcset/sizes as the lead image, to avoid
        shipping a flat, oversized image to every card on a mobile PLP.

        Gated on card_hover: a merchant who turns the cross-fade off should stop
        paying for the second photograph, not just stop seeing it. {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- if card_hover == 'second' and product.images[1] -%}
          <img
            class="card__alt"
            src="{{ product.images[1] | image_url: width: 640 }}"
            srcset="{{ product.images[1] | image_url: width: 420 }} 420w, {{ product.images[1] | image_url: width: 640 }} 640w, {{ product.images[1] | image_url: width: 900 }} 900w"
            sizes="(max-width: 720px) 50vw, 25vw"
            width="{{ card_w }}" height="{{ card_h }}" alt="" loading="lazy" aria-hidden="true">
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- else -%}
        {%- assign ph = product.id | modulo: 6 | plus: 1 -%}
        {{ 'product-' | append: ph | placeholder_svg_tag }}
      {%- endif -%}
    </a>

    {%- comment -%} ONE tag on the photograph, and the percentage only in the
    price line — never three prints of the same discount. Both tags share the
    same corner and are mutually exclusive by construction. {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if product.available == false -%}
      <span class="card__tag card__tag--sold lbl-sm">{{ 'product.atc.sold_out' | t }}</span>
    {%- elsif has_discount -%}
      <span class="card__tag card__tag--sale lbl-sm">{{ 'product_card.sale_flag' | t }}</span>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>

  {%- comment -%} Everything under the photograph in ONE box, so the stylesheet
  has a single element to rhythm against (the name/price stack and the
  quick-add were previously siblings of the card itself, which is why nothing
  could be aligned to anything). {%- endcomment -%}
  <div class="card__body">
    <a class="card__info" href="{{ product.url }}">
      {%- comment -%} NO CATEGORY LINE: the card is title · price · swatches.
      card_cat stays assigned above and the .card__cat CSS stays — restoring is
      one <p> again. {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if card_show_vendor and product.vendor != blank -%}
        <p class="card__vendor lbl-sm mute">{{ product.vendor | escape }}</p>
      {%- endif -%}
      <h3 class="card__name">{{ card_name | escape }}</h3>
      {%- comment -%} NO RATING ON THE CARD: settings.rating_value/rating_count
      are ONE store-wide pair, so printing them on every card would claim a
      per-product rating that does not exist — the same number under twenty
      different products reads as filler the moment anyone notices. It stays
      where it is true as a shop-level figure: the product page, the drawer and
      the homepage. The card keeps name · price.

      The CSS (.card__rating) stays in card-extras.css if this is ever
      restored. {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%} Three named parts, one formatting rule. `was` is the
      anchor, `now` is the price being charged, `off` is the real percentage
      computed from THIS product's own compare_at_price — an odd −36% reads as
      true in a way a round marketing number never does. {%- endcomment -%}
      <p class="card__price">
        {%- if has_discount -%}
          <s class="card__price-was">{%- render 'price', amount: product.compare_at_price -%}</s>
          <span class="card__price-now card__price-now--sale">{%- render 'price', amount: product.price -%}</span>
          <span class="card__price-off">&minus;{{ card_pct }}%</span>
        {%- else -%}
          <span class="card__price-now">{%- render 'price', amount: product.price -%}</span>
        {%- endif -%}
      </p>
    </a>

    {%- comment -%} The quick-add sits OUTSIDE `.card__info`'s anchor for the
    same reason the swatch row does: a button inside a same-page <a> fights the
    browser's own navigation on click. It keeps both theme.js hooks unchanged —
    only its position in the card moved. The glyph is decorative here because
    the label next to it already says the same thing; the aria-label names the
    product so a screen-reader user hears which one they are adding. {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if show_quick_add and product.available -%}
      <button
        type="button"
        class="card__add"
        data-quick-add
        {% if product.variants.size == 1 %}data-single-variant="{{ product.variants.first.id }}"{% else %}data-quick-view="{{ product.handle }}"{% endif %}
        aria-label="{{ 'product_card.quick_add_aria' | t: title: product.title }}"
      >
        <span class="card__add-icon" aria-hidden="true">{% render 'icon', name: 'cart', size: 17 %}</span>
        <span class="card__add-label lbl-sm">{{ 'product.atc.add_to_bag' | t }}</span>
      </button>
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%} Swatches live OUTSIDE `.card__info`'s anchor on purpose: the
    "+N" overflow is a real control — a checkbox/label pair that reveals the
    rest of the dots in place, CSS-only, no theme.js change. A checkbox inside a
    same-page <a> fights the browser's own navigation on click, so the whole
    swatch row is a sibling of the info link instead of a child of it. Dots
    ship colourless with data-swatch; see the file header for why. {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if card_swatch_option and card_swatch_option.values.size > 1 -%}
      {%- comment -%} No id/for pair here, deliberately. The obvious `<input id>` +
      `<label for>` version breaks the moment the SAME product is rendered twice on one
      page (a piece that sits in the bestsellers row and in a second homepage row, or in
      a related row on its own PDP): both cards emit the same id, and every browser
      resolves `for` against the FIRST one — so tapping "+3" on the second card silently
      expands the first. Wrapping the checkbox in its label needs no id at all and cannot
      collide. The reveal then hangs off `:has()` (Safari 15.4+, the same floor
      showModal() already sets for this theme). Where `:has()` is missing the extras just
      stay collapsed — degraded, never broken.

      aria-hidden sits on the individual dots, NOT on the row: the row now contains a
      real focusable control, and a focusable element inside an aria-hidden subtree is
      the classic screen-reader trap. {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%} REAL PHOTOGRAPHS, NOT COLOUR DOTS: a dot can only ever be an
      approximation of a printed floral or a two-tone knit, and a dot palette has
      to be maintained by hand besides. This uses the SAME source of truth the PDP
      already uses for its colour picker: the first product image whose alt text
      is tagged " in <Colour>," / " in <Colour>-", with the colour's own variant
      featured image as the fallback. Same rule, same result, no second list to
      keep in sync.

      ONE loop over all values, instead of a separate limit:4 / offset:4 pair —
      the lookup is not cheap enough to write twice, and the "+N" label is simply
      emitted after the fourth entry. {%- endcomment -%}
      <span class="card__swatches">
        {%- assign card_more_count = card_swatch_option.values.size | minus: 4 -%}
        {%- for value in card_swatch_option.values -%}
          {%- assign sw_i = forloop.index0 -%}

          {%- if sw_i == 4 -%}
            {%- comment -%} Both states are in the markup and CSS shows one. The first
            version hid the whole label once checked, which meant a keyboard user who
            opened the row lost focus into nowhere and had no way to close it again —
            the control deleted itself on use. {%- endcomment -%}
            {%- comment -%} No close state: the control is replaced by what it
            revealed — once the remaining colours are on screen the "+N" has
            nothing left to say, and the swatches it uncovered are links, so
            keyboard focus lands on real targets rather than nowhere. A reload
            collapses the row again. {%- endcomment -%}
            <label class="card__more lbl-sm">
              <input type="checkbox" class="card__more-toggle">
              <span class="card__more-open" aria-hidden="true">+{{ card_more_count }}</span>
              <span class="visually-hidden">{{ 'product_card.more_colours' | t: count: card_more_count, title: product.title }}</span>
            </label>
          {%- endif -%}

          {%- comment -%} A swatch the merchant configured in Shopify admin is a
          FACT; the alt-text convention below and the colour-name table in
          theme.js are guesses. A guess must never beat a fact — and until now
          neither was even read, so a store that set its swatches saw nothing
          change and had no way to find out why. {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- assign sw_img = value.swatch.image -%}
          {%- assign sw_down = value | downcase -%}
          {%- assign sw_needle = ' in ' | append: sw_down | append: ',' -%}
          {%- assign sw_needle2 = ' in ' | append: sw_down | append: '-' -%}
          {%- assign sw_needle3 = ' i ' | append: sw_down | append: ',' -%}
          {%- assign sw_needle4 = ' i ' | append: sw_down | append: '-' -%}
          {%- unless sw_img -%}
          {%- for m in product.media -%}
            {%- if m.media_type != 'image' -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endif -%}
            {%- assign sw_alt = m.alt | default: '' | downcase -%}
            {%- if sw_alt contains sw_needle or sw_alt contains sw_needle2 or sw_alt contains sw_needle3 or sw_alt contains sw_needle4 -%}
              {%- assign sw_img = m -%}
              {%- break -%}
            {%- endif -%}
          {%- endfor -%}
          {%- if sw_img == nil -%}
            {%- for pv in product.variants -%}
              {%- if pv.options[card_swatch_index] == value and pv.featured_media -%}
                {%- assign sw_img = pv.featured_media -%}
                {%- break -%}
              {%- endif -%}
            {%- endfor -%}
          {%- endif -%}
          {%- endunless -%}

          {%- comment -%} SELECT IN PLACE, NAVIGATE FROM THE IMAGE: tapping a
          swatch shows that colour inline on the card image; tapping the image
          then navigates to that colour's product page.

          The swatch stays an <a> to ?variant=<id> — with JS off it still works as
          a plain link. card-extras.js intercepts the click: it paints the swatch's
          data-card-swap photo into the card's lead image, keeps it (hover-restore
          returns to the SELECTED colour, not the shipped one), and repoints
          .card__art / .card__info at the variant URL — so the follow-up click on
          the image or title lands on that colour's product page. {%- endcomment -%}
          {%- assign sw_variant_id = '' -%}
          {%- for pv in product.variants -%}
            {%- if pv.options[card_swatch_index] == value -%}
              {%- assign sw_variant_id = pv.id -%}
              {%- if pv.available -%}{%- break -%}{%- endif -%}
            {%- endif -%}
          {%- endfor -%}
          <a
            class="card__swatch{% if sw_i > 3 %} card__swatch--extra{% endif %}"
            href="{{ product.url }}{% if sw_variant_id != blank %}?variant={{ sw_variant_id }}{% endif %}"
            title="{{ value | escape }}"
            aria-label="{{ product.title | escape }} in {{ value | escape }}"
            {% if sw_img %}data-card-swap="{{ sw_img | image_url: width: 600 }}"{% endif %}
          >
            {%- if sw_img -%}
              <img src="{{ sw_img | image_url: width: 96, height: 128, crop: 'center' }}" width="48" height="64" alt="" loading="lazy" decoding="async">
            {%- else -%}
              {%- comment -%} no photograph tagged for this colour yet — fall back to
              the old dot so the row never shows a hole {%- endcomment -%}
              {%- if value.swatch.color -%}
                <span class="card__swatch-dot" data-swatch="{{ value | escape }}" data-swatch-set style="background-color: {{ value.swatch.color }};"></span>
              {%- else -%}
                <span class="card__swatch-dot" data-swatch="{{ value | escape }}"></span>
              {%- endif -%}
            {%- endif -%}
          </a>
        {%- endfor -%}
      </span>
    {%- endif -%}
  </div>
</article>
